---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Treadmill Parts (2026): Where to Buy, OEM vs Aftermarket & What They Cost";
const description =
  "Which treadmill parts fail, how to find your model and serial number, OEM against aftermarket, where to buy NordicTrack, ProForm, Sole, Life Fitness and Precor parts, and when a part costs more than the machine is worth.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "lube-n-walk",
    name: "Lube-N-Walk Treadmill Lubricating Kit",
    brand: "Lube-N-Walk",
    category: "The treadmill part you will buy most often",
    badge: "Buy First",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000AXCF9Q?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41s-n+6rcBL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":495},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$17–$25",
    priceBounds: {"low":17,"high":25},
    specs: "100% silicone · 24 in. applicator wand · refills sold separately · made in USA",
    pros: [
      "100% silicone, the lubricant most treadmill makers specify for the belt and deck",
      "The 24-inch wand reaches under the belt without removing it",
      "Refill bottles are sold separately, so the wand is a one-off purchase",
      "Cheap insurance against the friction that wears belts, boards and motors",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Some decks are pre-waxed and must not be lubricated; check your manual first",
      "Lube-N-Walk also sells a wax version; buy the one your manual specifies",
      "A little more expensive than a plain bottle of silicone",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Buy this before any other part, and use it on the schedule in your manual; it prevents more failures than it fixes.",
    description: "Belt lubricant is a part in all but name: it is the consumable that keeps the walking belt sliding over the deck, and running without it raises friction that wears the belt and deck and strains the drive motor and control board. Most treadmill makers specify 100% silicone, and our lubricant guide explains why petroleum products and general-purpose sprays damage belts. Lube-N-Walk's kit is 100% silicone, and its main advantage over a plain bottle is the 24-inch flexible wand, which slides under the belt and reaches the centre of the deck without loosening anything. Retailers describe the system as made in the USA since 1998, refill bottles are sold separately, and TreadmillReviewGuru lists it among its preferred treadmill lubricants. For scale, FitnessRepairParts listed iFIT’s own treadbelt lubricant for a NordicTrack at $12.00 on 5 November 2026. Check your manual first: some decks are pre-waxed and should not be lubricated at all, some makers specify their own product, and Lube-N-Walk sells a wax version for pre-waxed decks, so buy the one your manual calls for. Figures are those checked for our treadmill accessories guide; our guide to how often to lubricate covers the schedule.",
  },
  {
    id: "universal-safety-key",
    name: "Universal Magnetic Treadmill Safety Key",
    brand: "",
    category: "A lost or weak safety key",
    badge: "Cheapest Fix",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GSZJ9CQG?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41oTg06HIcL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: "/treadmill-safety-key-not-working/",
    priceRange: "Amazon price not confirmed on 5 Nov 2026",
    priceBounds: null,
    specs: "Magnetic key on a cord and clip · fits many, not all, magnetic key ports",
    pros: [
      "The fix for the most common “treadmill will not start” call",
      "Universal magnetic keys fit many home treadmills that use a magnet in the key",
      "Often sold in packs, so you have a spare",
      "Restores the stop function, which you should never bypass",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Will not work on treadmills whose key uses a shaped plug or a specific magnet position",
      "Magnet strength varies between brands",
      "An OEM key by part number is the surer match",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Buy a universal key if yours is lost and your treadmill uses a simple magnetic key; buy the OEM key by part number if not.",
    description: "The safety key is the magnet or plug on a cord that clips to your clothing and stops the belt if you fall. A lost or worn key is the cheapest treadmill part and one of the most common reasons a treadmill will not start. Universal magnetic keys work on many home treadmills whose key is a simple magnet that sits on a sensor in the console; they do not fit consoles that use a shaped plug, a key with a specific magnet position or a coded key. For scale, FitnessRepairParts listed two NordicTrack safety keys at $6.97 and $19.55 on 5 November 2026. If a universal key does not work, look up the OEM key by your model number. Do not tape a magnet in place or bypass the key: the stop function is the treadmill’s most important safety feature, and our guide to safety key faults covers the other causes of a “no key” error.",
  },
  {
    id: "replacement-walking-belt",
    name: "Replacement Treadmill Walking Belt (Sized to Your Deck)",
    brand: "",
    category: "A worn, frayed or slipping walking belt",
    badge: "Measure First",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=treadmill+replacement+walking+belt&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: "/treadmill-belt-size-guide/",
    priceRange: "Amazon price not confirmed on 5 Nov 2026",
    priceBounds: null,
    specs: "Sold by length x width · single- or double-ply · pre-lubricated on some",
    pros: [
      "Aftermarket belts in common sizes are widely available and often cheaper than OEM",
      "A new belt can restore a treadmill whose only fault is a worn running surface",
      "Some come pre-lubricated, which suits a deck that has also been cleaned",
      "A good aftermarket belt is made to the original dimensions",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Must match your belt’s exact length and width, measured or by part number",
      "A worn deck under a new belt can wear the belt quickly; check both",
      "The heaviest DIY job on this page",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Worth buying when the belt is the problem and the deck and motor are sound; measure the old belt or use the part number.",
    description: "The walking belt is the largest wear part on a treadmill and one of the most often replaced. Aftermarket belts are sold by length and width, sometimes with a choice of ply and texture, and must match your machine exactly; our belt size guide explains how to measure an installed belt and why the length printed inside it is the number to trust. For scale, FitnessRepairParts listed NordicTrack C2000 walking belts at $126.71 and $130.32 on 5 November 2026, and our belt replacement cost guide gives the wider ranges for generic and OEM belts. Before buying, check the deck: if the belt has worn through, the deck surface may have worn too, and a new belt on a rough deck wears quickly. Our guide to replacing a treadmill belt covers fitting, tensioning and centring, which is where most DIY belt jobs go wrong.",
  },
  {
    id: "motor-drive-belt",
    name: "Treadmill Motor Drive Belt (Matched by Part Number)",
    brand: "",
    category: "A slipping or squealing drive belt",
    badge: "Common Cheap Fix",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=treadmill+motor+drive+belt&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: "/treadmill-drive-belt/",
    priceRange: "Amazon price not confirmed on 5 Nov 2026",
    priceBounds: null,
    specs: "Poly-V (ribbed) or cogged · matched by length, rib count and part number",
    pros: [
      "Cheap, and often the real cause of a belt that hesitates under each step",
      "On most treadmills, reachable by removing the motor hood",
      "Aftermarket drive belts are sold against OEM part numbers",
      "Worth replacing at the same time as a walking belt on an older machine",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Must match the profile, rib count and length exactly",
      "Some designs need the motor moved to fit and tension the new belt",
      "Easily confused with a slipping walking belt; diagnose first",
    ],
    bottomLine: "A $25-to-$35 part that fixes a lot of “my treadmill slips” problems; buy it by part number from your manual.",
    description: "The drive belt is the short ribbed or toothed belt between the motor pulley and the front roller. It stretches and glazes with use, and a worn one slips under load, which feels like the walking belt hesitating each time your foot lands. It is a cheap part: FitnessRepairParts listed two NordicTrack C2000 drive belts at $23.47 and $33.64 on 5 November 2026. Drive belts are model-specific in profile, rib count and length, so buy by the part or key number in your manual’s parts list, or by the numbers printed on the old belt. Aftermarket belts listed on Amazon often quote the OEM part number they replace; check that number rather than the brand in the title. Our drive belt guide explains how to tell a slipping drive belt from a slipping walking belt, and our guide to a belt that slows when you step on it covers the other causes.",
  },
  {
    id: "motor-brushes",
    name: "Treadmill DC Motor Carbon Brushes (Matched to Your Motor)",
    brand: "",
    category: "Loss of power on an older brushed DC motor",
    badge: "For Older Motors",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=treadmill+motor+carbon+brushes&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: "/treadmill-motor-brushes/",
    priceRange: "Amazon price not confirmed on 5 Nov 2026",
    priceBounds: null,
    specs: "Carbon brush pair · sized to the motor’s brush holder",
    pros: [
      "Can restore power to a brushed DC motor at a tiny fraction of a new motor’s cost",
      "Fitted from outside the motor on many designs, through brush caps",
      "Worth checking before replacing a motor or control board",
      "Usually sold in pairs",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Only for brushed DC motors; brushless and AC motors have none",
      "Must match the motor’s brush size and spring; the motor label is the guide",
      "A badly worn commutator may need a technician",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Worth a look on an older home treadmill that has lost power, before you buy a motor or a board.",
    description: "Many home treadmills use brushed DC motors, in which spring-loaded carbon brushes carry current to the spinning commutator. The brushes wear down over years, and worn brushes cause a loss of power, intermittent drive or sparking. On many motors they are accessible through caps on the motor body. Replacement brushes must match the motor’s brush dimensions and spring, so check the motor’s own label and model number rather than the treadmill’s; generic listings on Amazon give dimensions to match against. Brushless DC and AC motors, increasingly common on newer and commercial machines, have no brushes. For scale, a NordicTrack C2000 drive motor was listed at $172.44 to $228.36 at FitnessRepairParts on 5 November 2026, so brushes are worth ruling out first. Our motor brushes guide shows how to inspect them and when a worn commutator means a technician.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Maintenance", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/maintenance/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Parts", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-parts/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Where can I buy treadmill parts?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "From the treadmill’s maker for electronics and anything under warranty; from independent parts sellers such as FitnessRepairParts, Sears PartsDirect and Sportsmith, which list parts by the maker’s part numbers; and from Amazon for lubricant, universal safety keys and belts matched by size or part number. A local repair technician can also source parts and confirm the diagnosis.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Where do I find my treadmill’s model and serial number?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On a decal on the frame. NordicTrack and ProForm manuals show the decal’s location on their front cover; it is often low on the frame at the front or rear. Life Fitness treadmills usually carry it on the frame underneath, and Precor says its label is near the power cord entry and under the left rear of the frame. Photograph it once you find it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Where do I buy NordicTrack or ProForm treadmill parts?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Both are iFIT brands, and their manuals direct you to iFIT customer service to order parts, quoting the model number, serial number and the part’s key number from the manual’s parts list. Your iFIT account shows your warranty end dates. Out of warranty, FitnessRepairParts and Sears PartsDirect list many NordicTrack and ProForm parts by model and part number.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are aftermarket treadmill parts any good?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For belts, drive belts, lubricant and safety keys, a good aftermarket part made to the original dimensions is usually fine. For motor control boards, consoles, sensors and motors, use the maker’s part: they are matched to your machine, and some makers, Precor among them, say non-approved parts can void the warranty.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can home owners buy Life Fitness treadmill parts?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not through the Life Fitness Parts Store, which Life Fitness says is open only to domestic US commercial customers. Home owners should contact Life Fitness customer support with the serial number, or buy from independent sellers such as Sportsmith and FitnessRepairParts, which list Life Fitness parts by model and serial range.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it worth replacing a treadmill motor or control board?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On a well-built machine whose frame and belt are sound, often yes. On a budget folding treadmill several years old, a motor or board can approach half the price of a comparable new machine once labour is added, and that is the point to compare it with replacing the treadmill. Rule out cheap causes such as the drive belt, brushes and lubrication first.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-05",
      dateModified: "2026-11-05",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 5,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-parts/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/treadmill-parts/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "maintenance");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-05",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-05",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill parts",
      "treadmill replacement parts",
      "proform treadmill parts",
      "nordictrack treadmill parts",
      "sole treadmill parts",
      "life fitness treadmill parts",
      "precor treadmill parts",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Buy First: Lube-N-Walk Treadmill Lubricating Kit", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/maintenance/">Maintenance</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buying Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Treadmill Parts: Where to Buy the Right One</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Most treadmill repairs come down to one part: a belt, a key, a motor board. Getting the right one depends on a number printed on a sticker most owners have never looked at. Here is how to find it, which parts fail and what they cost, where each major brand sells its parts, and how to tell when a part is not worth buying.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 5, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#lube-n-walk"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41s-n+6rcBL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Lube-N-Walk Treadmill Lubricating Kit" width="500" height="495" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#universal-safety-key"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41oTg06HIcL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Universal Magnetic Treadmill Safety Key" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Lube-N-Walk Treadmill Lubricating Kit · Universal Magnetic Treadmill Safety Key</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Treadmill parts are not hard to buy. They are easy to buy wrong. The same model name can cover
          several versions built years apart with different motors, belts and boards, and the only reliable
          way to tell them apart is the model and serial number on the frame. Find that first, and the rest
          of this page is a matter of choosing who to buy from.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Buy First:</span> <a href="#lube-n-walk">Lube-N-Walk Treadmill Lubricating Kit</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Cheapest Fix:</span> <a href="#universal-safety-key">Universal Magnetic Treadmill Safety Key</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Measure First:</span> <a href="#replacement-walking-belt">Replacement Treadmill Walking Belt (Sized to Your Deck)</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Common Cheap Fix:</span> <a href="#motor-drive-belt">Treadmill Motor Drive Belt (Matched by Part Number)</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">For Older Motors:</span> <a href="#motor-brushes">Treadmill DC Motor Carbon Brushes (Matched to Your Motor)</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[4].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On 5 November 2026 we read, where they would load, the parts and support pages of iFIT (NordicTrack
          and ProForm), Sole, Life Fitness and Precor, those brands’ owner’s manuals, and the catalogues of
          three independent parts sellers (FitnessRepairParts, Sears PartsDirect and Sportsmith). NordicTrack’s
          and ProForm’s own parts pages returned “too many requests” errors and iFIT’s returned not found, so
          what we say about ordering their parts comes from their manuals and support material rather than
          a live parts store; Life Fitness’s and Sportsmith’s support pages were read through search results.
          The part prices we quote are one seller’s listed prices for one model on that day, to show the
          scale of costs, not quotes for your machine. Amazon’s pages would not load for us, so most product
          cards are generic parts with tagged Amazon searches and no prices; on 9 November 2026 the generic
          lubricant card was replaced by the Lube-N-Walk kit, with figures and prices as checked for our
          treadmill accessories guide, and the safety key card links a specific universal key. We have not fitted these parts.
          We searched the CPSC recall database for NordicTrack, ProForm, Sole, Life Fitness and Precor: none
          has a current treadmill recall (iFIT’s, Life Fitness’s and Precor’s recalls since 2015 are of rowers,
          dumbbells, a climber and a cable station). Repairs involve mains electricity: unplug the treadmill
          before opening any cover, and if you are unsure, use a technician. Prices change often. We earn a
          commission if you buy through the Amazon links on this page; it does not change what we say.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#at-a-glance">Treadmill Parts at a Glance</a></li>
            <li><a href="#picks">Commonly Bought Treadmill Parts on Amazon</a></li>
            <li><a href="#which-parts-fail">Which Treadmill Parts Fail, and Why</a></li>
            <li><a href="#model-and-serial">Find Your Model and Serial Number First</a></li>
            <li><a href="#oem-vs-aftermarket">OEM vs Aftermarket Treadmill Parts</a></li>
            <li><a href="#where-to-buy">Where to Buy Parts, Brand by Brand</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty-first">Check the Warranty Before You Buy Anything</a></li>
            <li><a href="#worth-fixing">When the Part Costs More Than the Treadmill Is Worth</a></li>
            <li><a href="#diy-or-technician">Fit It Yourself or Call a Technician?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Treadmill Parts at a Glance</h2>
<p>
            The parts that wear or fail most often, what they do, and the scale of what they cost. The prices
            are from one independent parts seller, FitnessRepairParts, for one home treadmill, a NordicTrack
            C2000, on 5 November 2026; parts for other machines differ, but the proportions hold.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Part</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical sign it has failed</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Listed price, one model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">DIY?</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Safety key</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lost, or the console will not start</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$6.97–$19.55</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt lubricant</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt drags, motor strains</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$12.00 (kit $24.85)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive (motor) belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Slipping, squealing, belt stops under load</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$23.47–$33.64</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Usually</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Frayed edges, worn surface, slipping</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$126.71–$130.32</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">With care</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor control board</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt will not move, erratic speed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$191.14–$470.02</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">If confident</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Burning smell, no drive, tripping power</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$172.44–$228.36</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Technician advised</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console (display)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Blank or dead buttons</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$392.04</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Usually</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two things stand out. The parts that fail most often, the key, the lubricant and the drive belt,
            are cheap. The ones that end a treadmill’s life, the motor, the control board and the console, can
            each cost a large share of what a budget machine sold for, before labour. Our <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a> covers diagnosis; this page is about buying the part once you know which it is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Commonly Bought Treadmill Parts on Amazon</h2>
<p>
            Amazon is a good place for the generic consumables and universal parts every treadmill uses, and
            a poor place for model-specific electronics, where a wrong match is easy and returns are slow.
            These five are the parts owners most often buy there. Each needs to match your machine; the card
            says how.
          </p>
        </div>
      <!-- Product blocks -->
