        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Where can I buy treadmill parts?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              From the treadmill’s maker for electronics and anything under warranty; from independent parts sellers such as FitnessRepairParts, Sears PartsDirect and Sportsmith, which list parts by the maker’s part numbers; and from Amazon for lubricant, universal safety keys and belts matched by size or part number. A local repair technician can also source parts and confirm the diagnosis.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Where do I find my treadmill’s model and serial number?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On a decal on the frame. NordicTrack and ProForm manuals show the decal’s location on their front cover; it is often low on the frame at the front or rear. Life Fitness treadmills usually carry it on the frame underneath, and Precor says its label is near the power cord entry and under the left rear of the frame. Photograph it once you find it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Where do I buy NordicTrack or ProForm treadmill parts?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Both are iFIT brands, and their manuals direct you to iFIT customer service to order parts, quoting the model number, serial number and the part’s key number from the manual’s parts list. Your iFIT account shows your warranty end dates. Out of warranty, FitnessRepairParts and Sears PartsDirect list many NordicTrack and ProForm parts by model and part number.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are aftermarket treadmill parts any good?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For belts, drive belts, lubricant and safety keys, a good aftermarket part made to the original dimensions is usually fine. For motor control boards, consoles, sensors and motors, use the maker’s part: they are matched to your machine, and some makers, Precor among them, say non-approved parts can void the warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can home owners buy Life Fitness treadmill parts?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not through the Life Fitness Parts Store, which Life Fitness says is open only to domestic US commercial customers. Home owners should contact Life Fitness customer support with the serial number, or buy from independent sellers such as Sportsmith and FitnessRepairParts, which list Life Fitness parts by model and serial range.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it worth replacing a treadmill motor or control board?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On a well-built machine whose frame and belt are sound, often yes. On a budget folding treadmill several years old, a motor or board can approach half the price of a comparable new machine once labour is added, and that is the point to compare it with replacing the treadmill. Rule out cheap causes such as the drive belt, brushes and lubrication first.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

